<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sétif</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sétif is a city on the high plains of north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algeria">Algeria</a>, capital of Sétif Province. It stands on the site of the Roman colony of Sitifis, capital of the late Roman province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mauretania-sitifensis">Mauretania Sitifensis</a>. On 8 May 1945 nationalist demonstrations there turned into riots that were followed by mass reprisals by French forces, an event that hardened Algerian nationalism.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/setif.jpg" alt="Sétif" width="320" height="176" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: BullyanDZ · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Park_Mall_setif.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Sétif lies in north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algeria">Algeria</a>, about 270 kilometres east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algiers">Algiers</a> and about 132 kilometres west of Constantine, in the Hautes Plaines region. It stands at an altitude of roughly 1,100 metres, which makes it the second-highest provincial capital in Algeria. The city sits between the Tellian Atlas to the north and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aures-mountains">Aurès</a> mountains to the south, on sedimentary rock of sandstones, marls and limestones.</p><p>Its streams, the Boussalem and El Kébir wadis, flow irregularly. The climate is continental with a strong <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> influence, with warm dry summers and cold, very rainy winters. The name comes from the Latin Sitifis, which is drawn from a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berber</a> word Zdif, meaning black lands, a reference to the fertility of the soil.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The city is of Numidian origin and was part of the kingdom of the Massaesyli in 225 BCE; it was small under the Numidian kings. Near Sétif the Numidian king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jugurtha">Jugurtha</a> campaigned and lost against Marius in 105 BCE. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> it became the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mauretania-sitifensis">Mauretania Sitifensis</a>. The first Muslim expedition to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> took place in 647, and by 700 the area had been conquered and converted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>. Little is known of the early Islamic town, but by the 10th century the area outside the fortress was again filled with houses, and over a dozen with large courtyards were excavated on the site of the Roman baths.</p><p>A column of the French colonial army took the city in 1838, as part of the operations against Constantine. At first the occupation was military, but civilians soon arrived. A first urban plan was set up by decree in 1843, a European city was laid out in 1846, and on 11 February 1847 a royal decree created it with an endowment of 2,509 hectares of land.</p><p>From 1850 it was a centre of colonial settlement, drawn by the farming potential of the region. The protests and massacre of 8 May 1945 began in the city and were a crucial factor in the start of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algerian-war">Algerian War</a>. The municipality was created at the start of independence, on 5 July 1962.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Sétif is the capital of Sétif Province and is regarded as one of the most important trading and industrial cities of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algeria">Algeria</a>. Agriculture in the region rests on grain and livestock, and local wheat is turned into semolina, couscous and noodles. One of its main historical monuments is the Ain El Fouara fountain, a sculpture by the French sculptor Francis de Saint-Vidal that was brought to the city at the end of the 19th century and was classified as national heritage on 3 November 1999. The local cuisine includes the couscous dish Berboucha, and a music genre called Staifi, based on the zendali rhythm, is popular across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maghreb">Maghreb</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Sétif” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>On 8 May 1945 demonstrations at <b>Sétif</b> turned into riots in which about a hundred Europeans were killed, and the army, air force and settler militias answered with reprisals around Sétif and Guelma that killed thousands of Algerians; estimates range from a few thousand to tens of thousands.</blockquote><a href="/empire/french-algeria">French Algeria · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li></ul></section>
